# A148 — an image is measured before it is decoded (2026-09-16)

## What the code did
# `ImageExtractor.wireRepresentation` decoded first and looked afterwards:
#     CGImageSourceCreateImageAtIndex(source, 0, nil)      // the whole canvas, in memory
#     … then properties, then re-encode, then `converted.count <= maximumFileBytes`
# The byte cap cannot bound that: the formats that reach this path are the ones a model cannot be given
# as they are (TIFF, BMP, HEIC) and they are compressed.

## The amplification, measured
# Valid single-strip PackBits TIFFs, written by the fixture generator: each row is a run of 128
# identical bytes, so the file is a fraction of the canvas it declares. ImageIO reports the canvas from
# the file's own metadata (`AUDIT/baseline/swift64/a148-probe/reported-size.swift`), decodes it, and
# re-encodes it as JPEG:

file        declared        on disk     decoded RGB      ratio    re-encoded   old code
small.tiff   20 × 20          4 230 B        1.2 KB         0.3×       —         converted
over.tiff  6 500 × 6 500    663 146 B      127 MB          191×      504 KB      converted
# and the same trick with PNG, which is not converted but shows what the byte cap allows:
bomb.png  12 000 × 12 000   441 252 B      432 MB          979×         —         —
# At the 64 MB byte cap, a crafted PNG is a canvas of tens of gigabytes. That is the bomb: nothing
# about it is slow or malformed, it is a small file that expands.

## The fix
# The dimensions come from `CGImageSourceCopyPropertiesAtIndex` — metadata, no pixels — and are checked
# against `AttachmentLimits.maximumImagePixels` (40 MP, new, more than twice a 20 MP camera) before
# `CreateImageAtIndex` is called at all. An image that will not report its dimensions is refused rather
# than decoded to find out, and the product is computed with `multipliedReportingOverflow` because a
# header may declare two numbers whose product traps.
#
# Images that need no conversion are untouched by this: they are passed through as they arrived, and the
# byte cap is what bounds them. There is no decode to bound.

## The tests, against both behaviours
# `AuditS3ImageDecodeTests` writes its own PackBits TIFFs, so what is refused is a format the app
# genuinely converts.

$ swift test --filter ImageDecodeTests          # with the fix
  ✔ Test "An image whose metadata declares too many pixels is refused without being decoded"
  ✔ Test "The cap is the caller's, and an image under it is still converted"
  ✔ Test "An image that will not report its dimensions is refused rather than decoded blind"
  ✔ Test "A file that is not an image at all is refused as that, not as an unmeasurable image"
  ✔ Test run with 4 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.009 seconds.

$ swift test --filter ImageDecodeTests          # with the decode put back before the check
  ✘ Test "An image whose metadata declares too many pixels is refused without being decoded"
      Issue recorded: a 42 MP TIFF was converted
  ✘ Test "The cap is the caller's, and an image under it is still converted"
      Expectation failed: an error was expected but none was thrown
  ✘ Test "An image that will not report its dimensions is refused rather than decoded blind"
      Expectation failed: reason.contains("dimensions")
  ✘ Test run with 3 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.042 seconds with 3 issues.

# The boundary case is why the cap is a parameter: a cap of 399 pixels refuses a 20 × 20 image and 401
# converts it, so what is tested is the cap rather than a hard-coded size. The first is the finding:
# 42 MP, 663 KB on disk, accepted by the old code after allocating 127 MB.

## Two refusals, told apart
# `CGImageSourceGetType` is the difference between "not an image at all" (nil: the file a picker offered
# because of its extension) and "damaged" (a recognised format whose size will not be reported). They are
# different sentences because they are different problems, and an existing test
# (`AuditAttachmentMediaTests`, "An image that cannot be decoded is refused with its name") pins the
# first — it failed when both cases shared the dimension message, which is how the distinction was found
# rather than reasoned about.

